therwise  [Music]  that feeling you get when you're out on  the track  for me it's what i live for  you get it banked into a corner and  you've got the bike drifting sideways  i'm out there and no one knows that i'm  in a wheelchair no one knows  you're just one of the guys you're a  sidecar racer you're not a disabled guy  and it's just it's an amazing feeling  [Music]  it's something that you feel a  connection with people automatically i  don't think there's a better feeling you  can kind of move the night with the  music and the energy of the people can  also move me  it just really feels fulfilling  [Music]  hi i'm angel i'm dren  we're twin djs and producers  what makes a good dj is being observant  if i play one track and i'm just like  everybody really responded to that it's  going in your head in a split second  i've got 10 other checks that sound just  like that  we do switch off usually every 30  minutes  she can kind of like move off of what i  did  when we're not playing we're watching  the audience she'll be playing and i'll  come over and wrestle up and like play  that next i know when my twin has it  we'll be okay  we we've been the bra grew up in the  bronx new york and it's a really  jamaican area a lot of reggae playing  out in the streets all the time  in fifth grade we entered this program  that helps inner-city  youth that are generally underprivileged  we got very lucky but some kids don't  get as lucky we got through middle  school and high school and we got into  dartmouth and we both decided to go  there  so when we graduated we came back to the  city  i was playing with equipment on and off  i went home one day and i told her like  this is what we have to do  after you graduate from college nobody's  thinking you'll then go on to become a  d-day  it was one of those moments where you  had to be resolute in what you were  doing  there's no kick yet  [Music]  and then the bass hits i kind of like it  i think dj is definitely a predominantly  male industry  we've heard some negativity from  not even other djs but people and  audiences and i think it's been  surprising just because it doesn't feel  like djing should be a gendered thing i  take a burden upon myself to perform  better because  we are women we do get a lot of good  feedback from girls and women who want  to learn a deal who want to learn how to  dj  if someone told me that i would end up  being a professional dj with angel i  would not have believed it  when you follow what you like and you  follow your dreams it can take you  anywhere  [Music]  in southern pennsylvania there is a  battlefield a battlefield so important  that it is thought to be the turning  point of the american civil war this is  a story about that place  it's also a story about cats  thousands and thousands of little cats  [Music]  this is rebecca hi i'm rebecca and this  is her twin sister ruth hi i'm ruth and  we run a museum of dioramas that are  made with handmade civil war cats civil  war  cats the combination of a childhood love  for clay cats and a strong fascination  with the american civil war it started  out as a hobby that we just did as kids  putting around in our bedrooms  that bit of child's play led to a  full-fledged museum which opened in  2013.  [Music]  the two of us discussed how much do we  want to emphasize the cat thing because  we don't want to come off as cutesy cats  the idea is the stories of the men not  so much the fact that they're cats they  just are cats because  they're cats  incredibly each of these scenes is to  scale one eighth of an inch equals one  foot in cat feet and so each one of our  soldiers represents one soldier who  would have been on the field we'll  calculate the numbers that would have  been left in the regiment and we'll fill  it in with that many cats soldiers so  for example low around top we're going  to eventually have about 5 thousand  soldiers on it typically one like the  angle we figure took us about four and a  half years the little round top diorama  we figure is more of a five or six year  diorama  we really just want to make the civil  war more approachable i guess for people  so they can relate to the history and  have more of a connection with the men  and the women involved  now that the word of this cat museum is  out all the people who love cats come in  and now not only are they seeing our  dioramas of falling in love with the  five and a half thousand little furry  smiles smiling at them  but they're also learning the stories  and suddenly they have an appreciation  of gettysburg and the history just  because they came to see the cats  [Music]  when my little brother lucas was born my  parents were told that he wouldn't walk  or talk and the doctors even said he  might not make it to his 10th birthday  but today a few months away from his  10th birthday we're doing our 15th  triathlon together  my name is noah aldrich and this is my  brother lucas aldrich  lucas  hey guys i'm talking  [Music]  noah is 11 and lucas is nine and lucas  was born with a rare genetic neurologic  condition called listencephaly  he's unable to walk unable to talk he's  unable to feed himself but despite all  of his challenges he's the happiest  little boy that you'll ever meet  about four years ago our boys started  doing triathlons together and most cases  that wouldn't be a big deal a lot of  kids do triathlons but there's something  very special about the way our boys do  triathlons  [Music]  i pull lucas in a raft for the swim  portion  then i pull him in a bike cart during  the bike  and i push him in a running cart during  the run  i wanted to do this because lucas can't  do things like we can he can't really  play sports so this is something we  could do together  currently we're training for our 15th  triathlon which is this weekend  before the race i always feel nervous i  don't think i'll ever get over that i  just don't want to hurt lucas i don't  care if i win the race he just has to be  okay  [Music]  before the race lucas gets serious and  he gets his race face on and they're  ready to compete  [Music]  they swim around 200 yards  then they transition to a bike  they'll go usually about three to five  miles  and then they'll run about a mile  noah is strong physically and especially  mentally because he's truly only doing  these races for his brother  sometimes i feel like that it's gotta be  over soon right but i just hear lucas  that's how he says go faster  every day is precious with that little  boy  i try to soak in the experience because  we don't know how long the boys will be  able to do triathlons together any one  of them could be their last  what these boys accomplish gives me a  nice sense of pride  noah lucas for an inspiration people see  these two young boys and it gives them  hope and it gives them encouragement to  go out and tackle life challenges  [Music]  you

